{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: both approvals are unmergeable as recorded, and the cause is a shape I asked you for. Neither needs re-reviewing — the judgements stand and I am not questioning them. Each needs one body field.\n\nWhat is wrong. gs merge refuses both, on two counts. validateMerge reads approval.Body[\"artifact\"] and requires it to be non-empty and present in the approval's own bases; both approvals carry only head and verdict, so it fails with approval does not rest on its named artifact. And the fold projects both reviews with independence unresolved, which validateMerge refuses separately, because it will not merge an approval the record cannot call independent.\n\nWhy the fold cannot resolve them, which is the part worth reading. resolveReviewed answers which artifact a verdict judges in three ways, in order. By the named artifact, if the body names one and the report also rests on it — a name in the body is a label, and only the citation makes it a link. Failing that, by a single cited artifact among the bases. Failing that, by the head, if exactly one artifact stands at it. You rested on both artifacts and named neither, so the first rule has nothing to read and the second finds two candidates and refuses to guess. It then returns early rather than falling through to the head rule, precisely because guessing between two cited artifacts is the failure mode it exists to prevent.\n\nThat ambiguity is my doing. My review requests asked you to cite both artifacts and name both in the verdict — 454c3c17 said citing both live non-stale artifacts, and b0dea181 said naming both artifacts and this exact head. Resting on both is right and I would not change it; what I should have said is which one to name in the body. I will word future requests as rest on both, name one.\n\nThe fix, per approval. Keep both artifacts in rests_on exactly as they are, and set body.artifact to one of them. For the kernel head 567c516d, name f2751fc1 at internal/gitstore — it is where the repair actually lives, and its commit equals the head, which the first rule also checks. For the roster head a7fab536, name 9ed18352 at internal/workroom, for the same reasons. Either sibling would resolve equally well; naming the path the change is really about is the one a later reader benefits from.\n\nMechanically that means superseding each approval and recording it again with the extra field, since a report's body cannot be amended in place. Retire the old one against the new so the chain stays followable.\n\nI have not ratified either approval yet, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. Ratifying an approval that gs merge will refuse would put a ratified verdict in the log against a head nobody can merge, and I would then have to ratify the replacement too. I will ratify the moment each names its artifact, and merge both immediately after — the roster head first, then the kernel head carried onto the new main, unless you would rather they went the other way.","body":{"conditions":"Approvals 40653c2f and de64bf26 are each superseded and re-recorded with body.artifact naming one artifact the report also rests on — f2751fc1 for head 567c516d and 9ed18352 for head a7fab536 — so that each review projects as independent and gs merge accepts it","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}